		<ParagraphStyleRange AppliedParagraphStyle="ParagraphStyle/Caption">
			<CharacterStyleRange AppliedCharacterStyle="CharacterStyle/Default">
				<Br />
			</CharacterStyleRange>
		</ParagraphStyleRange>
		<ParagraphStyleRange AppliedParagraphStyle="ParagraphStyle/Page &gt; Running Text%3aImages%3aImg caption">
			<CharacterStyleRange AppliedCharacterStyle="CharacterStyle/Default">
				<Content>Fig. 2. BABY N</Content>
				<Br />
				<Content>The BNC connector is a miniature quick connect/disconnect radio frequency connector used for coaxial cable. It features two bayonet lugs on the female connector; mating is fully achieved with a quarter turn of the coupling nut. BNC connectors are attached to the ends of coaxial cables and can be used for connecting signals such as RF signals and video signals on consumer electronics. BNC stands for Bayonet Neill Concelman. Bayonet describes the physical connector type and Neill and Concelman are the names of the BNC connector&apos;s two inventors. The acronym BNC has been mistakenly believed to stand for British Naval Connector, Bayonet Nut Connector, and Baby N connector.</Content>
				<Br />
				<Content> BNCs are suited to accommodate a large variety of RG and industry standard cables, in a variety of termination styles.</Content>
				<Br />
				<Content>• Bayonet coupling mechanism provides positive, quick mating and un-mating</Content>
				<Br />
			</CharacterStyleRange>
			<CharacterStyleRange AppliedCharacterStyle="CharacterStyle/Default">
				<Properties>
					<Leading type="unit">7</Leading>
				</Properties>
				<Content>• 50 Ω and 75 Ω impedance designs allow customers to match impedance to system requirements</Content>
			</CharacterStyleRange>
			<CharacterStyleRange AppliedCharacterStyle="CharacterStyle/Default">
				<Properties>
					<Leading type="unit">9</Leading>
				</Properties>
				<Br />
			</CharacterStyleRange>
			<CharacterStyleRange AppliedCharacterStyle="CharacterStyle/Default">
				<Properties>
					<Leading type="unit">4</Leading>
				</Properties>
				<Content>• Connectors are available for military, industrial and commercial applications.</Content>
			</CharacterStyleRange>
			<CharacterStyleRange AppliedCharacterStyle="CharacterStyle/Default" Position="Superscript">
				<Properties>
					<Leading type="unit">4</Leading>
				</Properties>
				<Footnote>
					<ParagraphStyleRange AppliedParagraphStyle="ParagraphStyle/Page &gt; Running Text%3aNotes%3aFootnote">
						<CharacterStyleRange AppliedCharacterStyle="CharacterStyle/Default">
							<Content><?ACE 4?>	Amphenol, https://www.amphenolrf.com/connectors/bnc.html.</Content>
						</CharacterStyleRange>
					</ParagraphStyleRange>
				</Footnote>
			</CharacterStyleRange>
			<CharacterStyleRange AppliedCharacterStyle="CharacterStyle/Default">
				<Br />
				<Content>In earlier computer networks, BNC connectors with coaxial cables were used in Ethernet networks, but Ethernet networks are now more commonly connected by RJ45 connectors and CAT5-style cables.</Content>
			</CharacterStyleRange>
			<CharacterStyleRange AppliedCharacterStyle="CharacterStyle/Default" Position="Superscript">
				<Footnote>
					<ParagraphStyleRange AppliedParagraphStyle="ParagraphStyle/Page &gt; Running Text%3aNotes%3aFootnote">
						<CharacterStyleRange AppliedCharacterStyle="CharacterStyle/Default">
							<Content><?ACE 4?>	Sam N. Austin, What Are BNC Connectors Used For? https://itstillworks.com/bnc-connectors-used-5044736.html, accessed 12 March 2025.</Content>
						</CharacterStyleRange>
					</ParagraphStyleRange>
				</Footnote>
			</CharacterStyleRange>
		</ParagraphStyleRange>
	</Story>
